/**
 * Copyright © 2026 Blue Fairy Development. All rights reserved.
 *
 * The Alpine component behind widget.phtml, registered as Alpine.data('bfslcChat').
 *
 * WHY AN INLINE SCRIPT AND NOT web/js/chat.js: Hyvä loads Alpine as a deferred
 * module script (Hyva_Theme::page/js/alpinejs.phtml), and a component must be
 * registered on `alpine:init`, before Alpine walks the page. An inline script in
 * before.body.end is parsed before any deferred script runs; a separate deferred
 * file is not guaranteed to be. This is Hyvä's own convention.
 *
 * THE SCRIPT BODY IS STATIC. No PHP is interpolated between <script> and
 * </script> - the per-page data travels in widget.phtml's data-bfslc-config
 * attribute instead - so the body hashes to the same sha256 on every page, every
 * store and every visitor. That hash is pinned in etc/csp_whitelist.xml and
 * Test/Unit/Csp/InlineScriptHashTest fails if the two drift.
 *
 * THE BODY MUST SURVIVE MAGENTO'S HTML MINIFIER UNCHANGED (production mode with
 * dev/template/minify_html on). Magento\Framework\View\Template\Html\Minifier
 * leaves whitespace inside <script> alone but deletes every `//` comment, every
 * run of whitespace before `</`, and the space in `> <`. So the body contains
 * no line comments, no `</`, no `> <`, and nothing between its last character
 * and </script>. InlineScriptHashTest runs the real Minifier over this file and
 * asserts the hash is unchanged.
 *
 * CSP-BUILD ALPINE. Hyvä 1.3.11+ serves the Alpine CSP build when the store's
 * policy has no 'unsafe-eval' (theme-module CHANGELOG, 1.3.11 "Support strict
 * CSP mode"). That build's evaluator (alpine3-csp.js generateEvaluator) only
 * walks a dot path and calls the result if it is a function: no operators, no
 * arguments, no assignment, and a path segment that is `undefined` throws. Hence:
 * every directive names a property, getter or method; every message carries
 * every field it is read by (never undefined); x-model is not used (it assigns -
 * Hyvä documents @input + :value instead); and a method run from inside x-for
 * reads the loop item as `this.message`.
 *
 * Non-reactive state (the EventSource, timers, File objects) lives in the `rt`
 * closure, not on the component: Alpine wraps component properties in Proxies,
 * and calling a native method such as EventSource.close() or
 * FileReader.readAsDataURL() through a Proxy throws "Illegal invocation".
 *
 * Visitor and agent text is only ever placed with x-text (textContent).
 *
 * REAL TIME ONLY WHILE SOMEONE CAN SEE IT. The reply stream holds a PHP worker
 * for as long as it is open, so it is open only while the panel is open AND the
 * tab is visible. With the panel closed the widget polls every 30 seconds instead,
 * which is what keeps the unread badge current; with the tab hidden it does
 * nothing at all. Reopening (or showing the tab) reconnects from the newest
 * message id already held, so nothing sent in between is missed. While polling,
 * every tenth poll also asks for the conversation's status, because the
 * messages endpoint answers 200 for a closed conversation and would never say so.
 *
 * A STREAM CAN END ON PURPOSE. `event: idle` (nothing said for 30 minutes)
 * closes it; the open, visible panel then polls every 60 seconds instead (an agent
 * may still answer), and the closed panel keeps its 30-second unread poll. The
 * stream is tried again when the visitor sends, reopens the panel, shows the tab
 * or a poll brings a new message. A reconnect the server answers with idle again -
 * 200 + `event: idle`, or 204 from an older server (rt.wasIdle remembers that the
 * attempt followed an idle) - goes back to the 60-second poll, not the 3-second
 * fallback. `event: superseded` (another tab took this conversation's one stream
 * slot) closes it; this tab then polls every 30 seconds rather than take the slot
 * back and start a tug of war - until the visitor sends from it, which makes it
 * the tab being talked in. `event: reconnecting` (the server's
 * StreamRunner::EVENT_RECONNECTING: the stream's slot lease expired and could not
 * be taken again; the server has no other name for it) reconnects at once from
 * the newest id held, unless the stream lived under 10 seconds, which is treated
 * as a refusal (3-second poll, stream again in 60 seconds).
 *
 * DELIVERY STATUSES SETTLE LATER. The stream and the poll only bring new ids, but
 * the server retries a failed Slack post for about 48 minutes. So while one of the
 * visitor's messages is pending or failed (and was sent less than 60 minutes ago),
 * the open, visible panel re-fetches from the lowest such id every 30 seconds;
 * "delivered" is final, and so is "failed" once the server says it is no longer
 * retrying (retrying === false; a message without the field is taken as still
 * retrying, so it never offers a resend that could reach Slack twice).
 *
 * RETRY. Offered on a message the store refused outright, and on a text message
 * the server has given up on (it is sent again as a new message); never while the
 * server is still retrying, or Slack would receive it twice. Not offered where the
 * same bytes cannot succeed (FINAL_REFUSALS: the conversation's file limit, a file
 * too large, of a refused type or empty). After a 429 or 503 that said Retry-After
 * it stays disabled for that many seconds. A send that meets an ended conversation
 * (401/410) starts a new one and is resent once (goneRetried), with a note before
 * it saying so; that resend is tracked apart from a given-up Retry (resent), so it
 * never takes that Retry away.
 *
 * REFUSAL WORDING (the same rule as the Luma widget's refusalText): an upload
 * refusal with its own label shows that label; no answer at all is a connection
 * problem; a 5xx never shows the server's text (developer mode puts stack traces
 * and SQL there) - "unavailable" for 503, the generic line otherwise; a 4xx with
 * one of this module's bfslc_ error codes shows the server's own words (they say
 * what to fix, e.g. bfslc_invalid_email); any other 4xx gets its status's wording.
 *
 * EVERY RESPONSE IS CHECKED AGAINST THE TOKEN IT WAS ASKED WITH. A response that
 * arrives after the conversation was ended or replaced is dropped, so a late
 * answer for the old conversation can neither end nor feed the new one. Likewise
 * an ended conversation removes the stored token only if it is still its own
 * (forgetStored compares first): another tab may have stored a newer one.
 *
 * Every class string returned below is a literal in this file, so the
 * view/frontend/tailwind/module.css @source glob reaches them.
 */

